      "id": 1,
      "text": "Long Creek earned its name from the creek that winds through the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Creek%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1828, a band of Kickapoo from Oklahoma camped along the creek, trapping and fishing before settlers arrived.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Creek%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first settlers, arriving in 1828 from North Carolina, built log cabins and cleared land for farming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Creek%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A schoolhouse opened in 1834, serving as a place of worship until the first church was built in 1842.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Creek%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Long Creek flows through the village center, heading west to join Finley Creek at Lake Decatur, a Sangamon River reservoir.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Creek%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Route 36 skirts the northern edge of Long Creek, linking the village northwest to Decatur and east to Tuscola.",
